---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Privacy Policy — WifiHeatmap">
    <header class="w-full border-b border-cyan-500/10 bg-[#050814]/80 backdrop-blur-md sticky top-0 z-50">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-6 h-18 flex items-center justify-between">
            <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`} class="flex items-center gap-3">
                <img
                    src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/icon.png`}
                    alt="WifiHeatmap Icon"
                    class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl object-contain shadow-sm"
                />
                <span class="text-xl font-extrabold text-white tracking-tight">WifiHeatmap</span>
            </a>
            <a
                href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`}
                class="text-sm font-semibold text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 transition-colors"
            >
                ← Back to Home
            </a>
        </div>
    </header>

    <main class="pt-16 pb-24 bg-[#050814]">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-6 max-w-4xl">
            <div class="mb-12 border-b border-slate-800 pb-8">
                <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-emerald-950/60 border border-emerald-500/30 text-emerald-300 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
                    <span>100% Offline & Private</span>
                </div>
                <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-4">
                    Privacy Policy
                </h1>
                <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm">
                    Effective Date: September 2026 • WifiHeatmap for Android
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="space-y-10 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-base">
                <!-- Section 1 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">1. Core Privacy Commitment</h2>
                    <p>
                        WifiHeatmap is designed from the ground up to respect your digital privacy. We operate under a fundamental rule: <strong>Your network data, survey maps, and physical movements belong solely to you.</strong>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        WifiHeatmap does not collect, track, transmit, or monetize any personal data, device identifiers, or RF scan telemetry. The application operates 100% on-device with zero network transmissions or backend communication.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Section 2 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">2. No Cloud Storage or Remote Telemetry</h2>
                    <p>
                        WifiHeatmap functions entirely on-device:
                    </p>
                    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-400">
                        <li><strong>No Server Telemetry:</strong> The application does not communicate with any external servers, APIs, or cloud infrastructure.</li>
                        <li><strong>No Third-Party SDKs:</strong> There are no advertising frameworks, crash analytics, user behavior trackers, or proprietary analytics libraries integrated into the app.</li>
                        <li><strong>No Accounts:</strong> You do not need to register, provide an email address, or create an account to use any feature of WifiHeatmap.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <!-- Section 3 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">3. Android Permissions & How They Are Used</h2>
                    <p>
                        WifiHeatmap requests only the minimum Android permissions strictly necessary to scan Wi-Fi signals and calculate walking steps for indoor heatmaps:
                    </p>

                    <div class="space-y-4 pt-2">
                        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
                            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-300 font-mono mb-2">ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION & ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                <strong>Why it is needed:</strong> Starting with Android 6.0 and continuing through modern Android releases, the Android operating system classifies Wi-Fi beacon frame scanning as location-sensitive data (because nearby BSSIDs and Wi-Fi networks could theoretically be correlated to geographical coordinates). As an operating system requirement, Android will return empty scan results unless location permission is granted.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-emerald-400 text-xs mt-2 font-medium">
                                ✓ WifiHeatmap processes beacon frames strictly in local RAM to calculate channel graphs and signal levels. Your location is never logged or transmitted.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
                            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-300 font-mono mb-2">ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                <strong>Why it is needed:</strong> Used exclusively when you perform a walking heatmap survey on a floor plan. It enables Android's hardware step detector to support our Pedestrian Dead Reckoning (PDR) model, automatically plotting your footsteps on the map.
                            </p>
                            <p class="text-emerald-400 text-xs mt-2 font-medium">
                                ✓ Step data is only recorded during an active survey session on your device and is discarded when the session ends.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
                            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-300 font-mono mb-2">ACCESS_WIFI_STATE & CHANGE_WIFI_STATE</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                <strong>Why it is needed:</strong> Allows the application to inspect Wi-Fi connection parameters (SSID, BSSID, RSSI, channel frequency, link speed) and trigger Wi-Fi scans.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
                            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-cyan-300 font-mono mb-2">NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES</h3>
                            <p class="text-slate-400 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                                <strong>Why it is needed:</strong> On Android 13 (API level 33) and newer, this permission is declared with the <code class="text-cyan-300">usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation"</code> attribute to perform device-to-device Wi-Fi discovery while reaffirming that physical geolocation tracking is not conducted.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Section 4 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">4. Data Export & Storage</h2>
                    <p>
                        Any floor plan images you import or survey data and reports you export remain entirely on your device's local file storage. When you export a CSV or image report, the file is shared strictly through the native Android system share dialog under your explicit user control.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Section 5 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">5. Children's Privacy</h2>
                    <p>
                        WifiHeatmap does not collect information from anyone, including children under the age of 13. The application is completely safe for all audiences and contains no age-restricted content or monetization.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Section 6 -->
                <section class="space-y-4">
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight">6. Contact & Inquiries</h2>
                    <p>
                        If you have questions, feedback, or inquiries regarding this Privacy Policy or the security practices of WifiHeatmap, please reach out via our official Google Play Store app listing:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <a
                            href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.heymeowcat.wifiheatmap"
                            target="_blank"
                            rel="noopener noreferrer"
                            class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 font-semibold underline underline-offset-4"
                        >
                            View WifiHeatmap on Google Play
                        </a>
                    </p>
                </section>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>

    <Footer />
</Layout>
